## Why This Is Asked
This interview question tests dynamic programming with prefix-state tracking and solution reconstruction. The inner minimum calculation forces careful leftward scanning, providing a tangible beginner-to-intermediate challenge while remaining implementable in O(n²k) time.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over prefixes with block count tracking
- On-the-fly maintenance of minimum values over sliding windows
- Solution reconstruction via backpointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k), Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
      let currentMin = Infinity;
      for (let p = i; p >= t; p--) {
        currentMin = Math.min(currentMin, A[p-1]);
        const candidate = (dp[p-1][t-1] + currentMin * (i-p+1));
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          pre[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = pre[i][t];
    blocks.unshift([p, i]);
    i = p - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

